   1: # Shared invariants â€” every role reads this first
   2: 
   3: This file is the constitution. Each role has its own contract; these rules bind all of them.
   4: Where a contract and this file disagree, this file wins.
   5: 
   6: ## Attribution is derived, never declared
   7: 
   8: cwd â†’ git worktree â†’ branch â†’ exactly one item id â†’ walk `parent` upward for the chain.
   9: The branch is primary because it is coupled to closure: its pull request closes that item, so a
  10: wrong branch surfaces as a wrong closure on the board instead of hiding.
  11: 
  12: - A chain handed to you in a spawn prompt is a **hint to verify**, never a fact. No gate reads
  13:   spawn prompts, so a wrong value there would be stamped faithfully for the life of the item.
  14: - Attribution degrades, never blocks. The one thing it blocks is closing a requirement.
  15: - Every id printed to the founder carries a very short title in parentheses â€” two to five words,
  16:   a recall hint, never the board's full sentence. Strip internal codes and say what the thing is:
  17:   `AI4DEV-21 (fake Stripe, GitHub, Anthropic)`, never `(H5 vendor stand-ins)`.
  18: 
  19: ## The record is the memory
  20: 
  21: No context survives a phase boundary. Everything the next role needs is on disk and pushed:
  22: `PHASE-STATE.md`, the plan, the rulings, the distilled findings, the pull request.
  23: 
  24: - **Push at every phase boundary.** Death may be involuntary; the remote is the only durable
  25:   output. This is not insurance â€” it is how the relay transports work.
  26: - A correction that matters beyond one conversation lives only if written into a file every
  27:   session loads. A message to a running agent dies with it.
  28: 
  29: ## The usage window â€” the coordinator guards it, and only the coordinator
  30: 
  31: Anthropic reports how much of each usage window is spent. `statusline.ps1` snapshots those
  32: numbers on every status-line refresh â€” the status line is the only place Claude Code delivers
  33: them, hooks get a different payload â€” and `loop/work/window-gauge.ps1` reads the snapshot and
  34: returns `OK`, `PAUSE` or `UNKNOWN`.
  35: 
  36: - **The coordinator decides; nothing else may.** Read the gauge on **every** `FLOW` and `PULSE`
  37:   line. A second thing with authority to halt work is the same failure as a second way to close
  38:   work â€” and a brake inside an agent is one the founder cannot see.
  39: - **One line, and it is `PAUSE`. The line is 85 percent** (founder 2026-08-12, superseding the 90
  40:   of 2026-08-06 â€” only the number moved). There is no lower "start nothing new" band. Such a band
  41:   would have to be justified by what a sitting costs, which nobody has measured, so it could only
  42:   ever be a number that felt safe. Work starts whenever the gauge says `OK`.
  43: - **Three hooks enforce that standing line mechanically, and they decide nothing.** They are the
  44:   prompt stamp's class of machinery: they apply a decision the founder already made, at the
  45:   moment it bites, and they are incapable of anything else. `window-gate.ps1` (PreToolUse on the
  46:   `Agent` tool) refuses a SPAWN at the line; `window-alarm.cmd` (PostToolUse and
  47:   PostToolUseFailure) puts the verdict line in front of any model within one tool call;
  48:   `stamp-hook.ps1` puts it in front of the founder on every prompt. None of them can stop work
  49:   already running, none can choose who is refused, and none can be
  50:   turned off by the actor it denies. They speak â€” that is their whole function â€” but they only
  51:   ever repeat the founder's standing line and the reading, and a message is not a decision. The
  52:   coordinator remains the only decider â€” what the gate refuses is new work starting, never work
  53:   in flight, and every judgement about what to do next is still the coordinator's.
  54: - **A denied actor parks itself, and the denial travels up one level at a time.** The deny reason
  55:   carries the choreography, so no role has to remember it: write your state, commit and push,
  56:   report `PARKED â€” window low` to your spawner naming the item, the next phase and the pushed
  57:   head, and end. Your spawner then meets the same wall on its own next spawn and does the same,
  58:   until the conductor parks the item with a park note. Nobody waits inside a running agent.
  59: - **`UNKNOWN` at a hook allows and warns; it never denies.** A broken instrument is not a spent
  60:   window, exactly as attribution degrades rather than blocks. The per-tool alarm stays silent on
  61:   `UNKNOWN` by decision: a running subagent can neither act on it nor fix it, and the actors who
  62:   can act hear it at every spawn and every prompt.
  63: - **`PAUSE` means stop at the next boundary.** Ask each running conductor to park â€” write its
  64:   state, push, and end â€” and stop it outright only if it does not. Graceful first: an agent
  65:   killed mid-write leaves a record that lies about where the work got to.
  66: - **A sitting that meets the line mid-flight parks at its last committed work item.** This is
  67:   why the executor commits one commit per work item: with no reserve band, running out mid-phase
  68:   is an expected outcome rather than a failure, and the commits are what make it cost one work
  69:   item instead of a sitting.
  70: - **`UNKNOWN` reports loudly and does not halt.** No reading, an unparseable one, or one past the
  71:   staleness limit means the instrument is broken, not that the window is spent â€” and a broken
  72:   instrument is not a reason to stop working, exactly as attribution degrades rather than blocks.
  73:   Say it in the open and fix the sensor; never let it pass silently as `OK`.
  74: - **Parking and continuing â€” the exact shape.** Ask the running conductors to park, then run
  75:   `loop/work/window-wait.ps1` **as a background command**. Its exit re-invokes this session, so
  76:   the founder continues in the same conversation with its context intact: nothing is resumed,
  77:   nothing restarted, no scheduled task involved. Waiting costs nothing â€” a parked session makes
  78:   no API calls, which matters because the reason for waiting is that there is no budget.
  79: - **Its exit means "the window should be open", not "you have budget".** The wait is anchored on
  80:   the provider's stated `resets_at`, because the gauge is refreshed by the status line and a
  81:   parked session renders none â€” during the wait, the number it would poll is frozen. So the
  82:   FIRST TURN AFTER IT EXITS must re-read the gauge (that turn renders the status line, making it
  83:   the first genuinely fresh reading) and park again if it still says `PAUSE`. A reset time that
  84:   has already passed rolls forward, and a session limit is not the same thing as being out of
  85:   credit.
  86: - **Exit 1 is a report to the founder, never a licence to carry on.** It means the blocking
  87:   window is a weekly one, days away â€” a decision about what to do next, not something to sit
  88:   through.
  89: - **Release one item at a time** when the window reopens, re-reading the gauge between each.
  90:   Restarting every parked conductor at once can spend a fresh window before anyone looks.
  91: - **Resume is centralized in the coordinator, and it is a FRESH conductor that resumes.** The
  92:   conductor that parked an item has ended; it is not restarted and it is not waited for. The
  93:   coordinator reads the park note, spawns a new conductor for that item, and points it at the
  94:   next phase from the note. This is why the park note names the item, the next phase and the
  95:   verified pushed head: the note is the whole hand-off.
  96: 
  97: ## Writing about the founder
  98: 
  99: - **Quote exactly or do not use quote marks.** Typos, missing words and all. Polishing someone's
 100:   words and then presenting them as a quotation is fabrication at small scale.
 101: - **Cite a date only where a message exists on that day**, in the founder's local time
 102:   (transcripts are UTC; the founder is UTC+3, so a 21:00 UTC message is the next day).
 103: - **Never convert "the founder asked" into "the founder ruled."** A question that prompted a good
 104:   rule is provenance for the question, not for the rule.
 105: - **A rule that loosens the process needs a real, explicit founder ruling.** Tightening may be
 106:   proposed; loosening may not be inferred.
 107: 
 108: ## The tree
 109: 
 110: - One worktree per item, created by the conductor, shared by every role inside that item.
 111: - **One writer at a time.** Only the role currently holding the work writes; everyone else reads.
 112: - A sitting exits with `git status --porcelain` empty. Uncommitted work is invisible to
 113:   reviewers, to CI and to the merge â€” so if it is not committed, it does not exist.
 114: - Reviewer output lands ONLY in the item's artifacts directory â€” `loop/items/<item>/artifacts/`,
 115:   **inside the tree** â€” and never elsewhere in the tree while a gate is open. (Founder ruling
 116:   2026-08-09: it lived beside the tree until the Write tool's isolation guard collided with that
 117:   design and a shell fallback got flagged as a policy bypass; inside the tree, no boundary is
 118:   crossed at all.) The fix and audit sittings commit the raw critiques, stderr logs and
 119:   distillates into the record at each phase boundary â€” the commit is what preserves evidence,
 120:   and it also marks the worktree changed so the platform will not auto-clean it under a dying
 121:   agent.
 122: - **A branch belongs to the DIRECTORY, not to the session.** Several sessions can sit in one
 123:   folder, and a branch change there moves all of them mid-turn, silently. So the main worktree
 124:   stays on `main` permanently and is where the coordinator works; every item gets its own folder
 125:   via `git worktree add`, with its own session opened in it. `loop/work/guard-branch-switch.ps1`
 126:   refuses a branch change in the main worktree so this cannot be forgotten mid-task rather than
 127:   merely written down here.
 128: - **Stale-worktree sweep** (founder 2026-08-05). A platform worktree is residue only when its
 129:   lock pid is DEAD, its tree is CLEAN, and its head is FULLY MERGED to main â€” then unlock,
 130:   remove, and delete the merged remote branch. **Check those three in that order**: a live agent
 131:   or unpushed work must never be swept, and a LIVE lock pid is never touched at all, because
 132:   force-removing a locked worktree is the hazard that breaks a resumed agent.
 133: 
 134: ## Committing
 135: 
 136: - **`.gitignore` ignores ALL of `.claude/` and `loop/out/`** (lines 45 and 53, no negations).
 137:   Already-tracked files there stay tracked â€” which is why those directories look normal â€” but a
 138:   NEW file under either path silently does not stage: it needs `git add -f`, then a verified
 139:   `A` line in `git status --short`. A commit once came one step from deleting eight skills
 140:   while adding zero replacements exactly this way. `loop/items/` is NOT ignored and stages
 141:   normally. Platform runtime paths (`worktrees/`, `checkpoints/`, `mailbox/`, the agent
 142:   registry) are excluded via `.git/info/exclude` on purpose and must never be force-added.
 143: - Commits cite the item they belong to.
 144: - **A closing verb beside an item id closes it.** `close`, `fix`, `resolve`, `complete`,
 145:   `implement` and their inflections act when they sit next to an id in a pull request title or
 146:   body â€” a careful post-mortem once closed a live item mid-work. Write "the instruction that
 147:   repaired X", "motivated by X", or use `ref` / `part of` / `towards`. CI enforces this.
 148: 
 149: ## RE-MEASURE A NEGATIVE WITH A DIFFERENT INSTRUMENT BEFORE BELIEVING IT
 150: 
 151: A negative result â€” "the file is empty", "no check exists", "the merge did not succeed", "the
 152: reviewer wrote nothing" â€” is the cheapest thing in this system to get wrong, because a broken
 153: instrument and a true absence produce identical output. AI4DEV-57 alone produced five:
 154: 
 155: - a merge that HAD succeeded reported as "no commit SHA exists", because `gh`'s branch cleanup
 156:   failed afterwards and the failure was read as the merge failing;
 157: - a broken `jq` query reporting no CI check for six minutes while it had gone green in 37 seconds;
 158: - a reviewer's output read mid-run and declared "written to the wrong stream", when it simply had
 159:   not finished;
 160: - mangled em-dashes surviving an entire green CI run undetected;
 161: - a phantom byte-order mark and corrupted indentation in a captured diff.
 162: 
 163: So: **before reporting that something is absent, failed, or empty, measure it a second way.** Ask
 164: the process, not the file. Ask the API, not the local cache. Read the error log, not the exit
 165: code. If the second instrument agrees, the negative is real and worth acting on; if it disagrees,
 166: you have just saved hours. Every one of the five above was caught by looking again with a
 167: different tool, and every one of them was reported confidently the first time.
 168: 
 169: ## Never
 170: 
 171: - Never merge without the required CI check green on the exact head the merge ruling pins.
 172:   Branch protection having `strict` and `enforce_admins` off is a configuration convenience,
 173:   not a licence â€” **for every role except the founder.**
 174: 
 175:   **The founder may bypass the check deliberately** (founder 2026-08-07). `enforce_admins` is
 176:   false, so `gh pr merge <n> --squash --admin` lands a pull request whose check is red, absent, or
 177:   never dispatched. That is the founder's call to make and no agent may make it, propose it as
 178:   routine, or treat a past bypass as precedent for the next one.
 179: 
 180:   **A bypass is only legitimate when it is recorded**, because a merged commit carries no trace
 181:   that nothing checked it â€” the merge looks identical either way, and six months later the
 182:   history claims a green that never existed. Before merging, a comment on the pull request states:
 183:   that the check was bypassed, why (dispatch outage, check unavailable, founder ruling), what
 184:   evidence stands in its place, and the exact head SHA that evidence was gathered against. If the
 185:   head moves afterwards, the evidence describes a different commit and must be regathered.
