  39: ## [T1] HIGH â€” the verdict/snapshot pair is not serialized â€” **ACCEPT**
  40: 
  41: > "The verdict/snapshot pair is not serialized, so concurrent status-line refreshes can leave a
  42: > high snapshot beside an older `OK` verdict." â€” terra, `loop/work/statusline.ps1:95`
  43: 
  44: **Real.** `Get-WindowDir` (window-lib.ps1:38-41) resolves to
  45: `%LOCALAPPDATA%\ai4good-build\nirdrang-ai4good` with no session scoping, Claude Code spawns a
  46: fresh PowerShell per status-line refresh, and several sessions at once is this project's normal
  47: operating condition â€” the session-prefix rule exists precisely because two sessions' output sits
  48: side by side in one view. So two writers on the same two files is real, not hypothetical.
  49: 
  50: The verdict-first single-`try` envelope makes the pair consistent for ONE writer and does nothing
  51: across two. This interleaving leaves a high snapshot beside an `OK` verdict:
  52: 
  53: ```
  54: A writes verdict(ALARM)  ->  B writes verdict(OK)  ->  B writes snapshot(low)  ->  A writes snapshot(high)
  55: ```
  56: 
  57: **The practical severity is lower than the finding implies, and I record that plainly.** Both
  58: decision-bearing checkpoints COMPUTE from the snapshot and never read the verdict line â€” the gate
  59: by D4, the stamp by D6. A stale line therefore cannot let a spawn through and cannot hide the
  60: alarm from the founder. The only affected reader is the per-tool alarm, and it self-heals at the
  61: next refresh, seconds later.
  62: 
  63: **It is accepted anyway, because the record claims impossibility.** statusline.ps1:74-79 says "is
  64: not defended against here - it is impossible", and plan D3 says "the invariant holds by
  65: construction". A false statement about the code is never mergeable, and this item's entire subject
  66: is a guard that must never look like a clear window when it is not.
  67: 
  68: **Fix.** Serialize the pair with a named system mutex `Global\ai4good-window-sensor`, acquired
  69: with a short timeout. On timeout, skip BOTH writes: another refresh is writing the same
  70: account-wide numbers, and a consistent pair one refresh old is strictly better than an
  71: inconsistent pair, because the staleness rules handle the first correctly and are defeated by the
  72: second. Tolerate `AbandonedMutexException` â€” it means we hold the lock. Release in a `finally`.
  73: The block stays inside the existing never-throw envelope, so a mutex failure degrades to "no
  74: sensor update", which the stale rules then report as UNKNOWN â€” loud, not silent.
  75: 
  76: Correct statusline.ps1's comment and plan D3 to state what is now guaranteed and what residual
  77: remains (a refresh may be skipped under contention; it heals at the next one).
  78: 
  79: **Verification condition.** A deterministic drill assertion, not a race: hold the mutex in the
  80: drill, run the sensor, assert it exits 0 and leaves the existing pair unchanged and uncorrupted;
  81: release, run again, assert both files update together.
  82: 
  83: ## [T2] HIGH â€” `SilentlyContinue` masks a library-load failure â€” **REJECT** (disproven first-hand)
  84: 
  85: > "`SilentlyContinue` can suppress a failed library load or missing `Get-WindowVerdict` command
  86: > before the outer `catch`, causing a silent exit 0 instead of the promised fail-open warning."
