 340: # ADDENDUM â€” step 10, ruled after the executor's first pass
 341: 
 342: The executor applied all 13 accepted fixes, reached green on every suite, and then STOPPED on
 343: step 10 and asked me rather than deciding. That was correct, and the case it found is genuinely
 344: outside what I pre-decided.
 345: 
 346: ## What it found
 347: 
 348: `settings-proof-probe.ps1:64` builds the probe's twin settings file like this:
 349: 
 350: ```powershell
 351: $twinText = $raw.Replace(($mainPrefix -replace '\\', '\\\\'), ($repo -replace '\\', '\\\\'))
 352: ```
 353: 
 354: `-replace` is the REGEX operator. The pattern `'\\'` matches one backslash, but in a .NET
 355: replacement string a backslash is literal, so `'\\\\'` inserts FOUR. The probe therefore searched
 356: the settings file for `C:\\\\Users\\\\nirdr\\\\...` while the file holds `C:\\Users\\nirdr\\...`.
 357: Nothing matched, and the twin came out a byte-for-byte copy of the committed file, still carrying
 358: MAIN-checkout paths. I confirmed this by reading line 64 myself.
 359: 
 360: `window-gate.ps1` and `window-alarm.cmd` do not exist in the main checkout yet, so two of the
 361: entries pointed at absent files. The probe's own precheck caught it â€”
 362: `FAIL every command in the twin points at a file that exists` â€” and the executor recorded nothing
 363: as an entry failing to fire.
 364: 
 365: ## Ruling â€” ONE re-run is authorised, and it does not widen the cap
 366: 
 367: **This is not the contingency I pre-decided, and the cap I wrote does not reach it.** That cap
 368: was aimed at a degraded vendor: it existed to stop the executor burning attempts against an
 369: unbounded external failure, and to stop a vendor failure being misrecorded as a hook entry not
 370: firing. The vendor was healthy here â€” five runs, every stderr log empty, no 529.
 371: 
 372: The decisive point is narrower than "the cap was for something else". **The probe never put the
 373: proposition under test.** It exercised a settings file that was not the branch's settings. That is
 374: not a failed attempt at the hypothesis; it is a setup that never presented the hypothesis. This is
 375: exactly the shape of the CI-unavailable class in my own contract: a run with no runner assigned
 376: and zero steps executed is an ABSENT signal, not a red one, and the answer is to obtain the
 377: signal â€” never to record a failure that was never observed. Counting this as the one attempt would
 378: put "we tried and could not prove it" in the record when the truth is "we have not yet tried."
 379: 
 380: **The vendor cap stands unchanged at one attempt.** If the re-run fails vendor-side, that IS the
 381: pre-decided contingency: record it blocked and stop.
 382: 
 383: ## Bounds on the re-run
 384: 
 385: 1. Fix line 64 only â€” plain string `.Replace('\','\\')` on both operands. Nothing else in the twin
 386:    logic changes.
 387: 2. **The precheck is the gate.** If "every command in the twin points at a file that exists" still
 388:    fails, STOP and report. Never run the headless cases against a twin that is still wrong â€” that
 389:    guard already earned its keep once.
 390: 3. ONE re-run of the five cases.
 391: 4. **Record what the run actually shows, including a genuine non-firing entry.** That would be a
 392:    real finding and it comes back to me, not into the record as a conclusion.
 393: 5. D11's contingencies are already ruled and stand: `PostToolUseFailure` not dispatched â†’ drop
 394:    that ONE entry, keep the rest, record the residual gap. `additionalContext` invisible on an
 395:    allow â†’ fall back to `systemMessage` only and record the reduced guarantee.
 396: 6. `goal-evidence.md` and `plan.md` then state the truth of the re-run, whatever it is. Step 9's
 397:    status moves only if its own criterion is genuinely met.
 398: 
 399: Fixing the probe is not scope growth: step 10 is in the plan, the probe is the plan's own
 400: artifact, and a one-line repair to make it run is completing planned work.
 401: 
 402: ## Recorded, because the executor was right to raise it
 403: 
 404: The executor found a new defect while working and reported it instead of fixing it silently, and
 405: it declined to re-run under a cap it could have read loosely. It also threw away two overhead
 406: measurements that were wrong for reasons it could name â€” a reporting function that both printed
 407: and returned, and twins that had crashed because `$PSScriptRoot` resolved to TEMP â€” and it refused
 408: to measure the pre-item status line by running it, because that version ignores the override and
 409: twenty-one runs would have written synthetic readings into the founder's live snapshot. That is
 410: this item's own incident, avoided by the executor unprompted.
