 414: # ADDENDUM 2 â€” the alarm entries did not deliver. This is OUR defect, and it must be fixed before merge.
 415: 
 416: The probe re-run came back 10 of 12. It PROVED three things at runtime, one of them better than
 417: the plan expected:
 418: 
 419: - the spawn gate DENIES against the deployed entry shape, with window, percent, reset and the
 420:   parking choreography in the reason â€” the item's core mechanism, proven;
 421: - `additionalContext` IS visible to the model on an allow, so D11's `systemMessage`-only fallback
 422:   is not needed and the full guarantee stands. **Contingency closed.**
 423: - `UserPromptSubmit` DOES fire headless and carries the founder line. The limit this item nearly
 424:   recorded as measured â€” "UserPromptSubmit may not fire headless" â€” is DISPROVEN. That was only
 425:   knowable because ruling [T4]/[F1] gave the stamp case its own over-the-line run; against the old
 426:   UNKNOWN transcript it could never have passed. **The finding paid for itself.**
 427: 
 428: **Both alarm entries delivered nothing â€” `PostToolUse` and `PostToolUseFailure` alike.** The
 429: executor established, before calling it a negative, that a real tool call happened in both runs,
 430: that the synthetic reading reached the child processes (the gate denied at 95% in the SAME runs),
 431: that `window-alarm.cmd` works when invoked as `cmd /c "<path>"`, and that every other entry in the
 432: same twin fired. It then declined to guess between "not dispatched" and "dispatched but the exit-2
 433: stderr was not surfaced", and brought it to me. Correct.
 434: 
 435: ## The ruling â€” it is neither of those two hypotheses, and I am not deferring it
 436: 
 437: I read `.claude/settings.json` myself. The correlation is total and it points at us:
 438: 
 439: | entry | invocation | delivered? |
 440: |---|---|---|
 441: | PreToolUse â†’ guard-branch-switch | `powershell -NoProfile â€¦ -File "â€¦"` | yes |
 442: | PreToolUse â†’ window-gate | `powershell -NoProfile â€¦ -File "â€¦"` | **yes** |
 443: | UserPromptSubmit â†’ stamp-hook | `powershell -NoProfile â€¦ -File "â€¦"` | **yes** |
 444: | SessionStart | `powershell -NoProfile â€¦ -File "â€¦"` | yes |
 445: | PostToolUse â†’ window-alarm | `"C:\â€¦\window-alarm.cmd"` â€” **no interpreter** | **no** |
 446: | PostToolUseFailure â†’ window-alarm | `"C:\â€¦\window-alarm.cmd"` â€” **no interpreter** | **no** |
 447: 
 448: Every entry that names an interpreter fired. The only two that do not name one are the only two
 449: that did not. The executor's own measurement fits: the batch file works under `cmd /c`, and the
 450: bare quoted path was never tried the way the hook runner would actually invoke it.
 451: 
 452: **The MECHANISM is a hypothesis, and I am marking it as one rather than letting it stand as fact.**
 453: My proposed explanation is that a hook command runs through a shell which cannot execute a `.cmd`
 454: file directly, and which may consume the backslashes in the path. **I tried to verify that locally
 455: and could not**: the `bash` on this machine's PATH is
 456: `C:\Users\nirdr\AppData\Local\Microsoft\WindowsApps\bash.exe`, a WSL stub that mangles Windows
 457: paths, so it is not the hook runner's shell and nothing it does is evidence about one. An earlier
 458: draft of this addendum asserted the shell mechanism outright. That was me stating an unverified
 459: fact in a ruling â€” the same defect I accepted [T1] and [F6] for â€” and this paragraph is the
 460: correction.
 461: 
 462: **The RULING does not depend on the mechanism.** It rests on the correlation, which is
 463: observational and comes from the real runtime: entries with an explicit interpreter deliver, this
 464: one does not. Wrapping it in an interpreter is justified by that alone, and the re-run is what
 465: confirms it empirically. If the corrected entry delivers, the fix is proven whatever the
 466: underlying cause was.
 467: 
 468: **So this is not an instrument limitation and not an unprovable absence. It is a defect in our own
 469: wiring, it is reachable, and it would have shipped a checkpoint that silently never runs** â€” which
 470: is precisely the failure class this whole item exists to remove. It does not get deferred to a
 471: post-merge check.
 472: 
 473: ## What the executor does â€” one invocation, a decision tree, at most one probe run
 474: 
 475: 1. **Cheap local diagnosis first, no model calls.** Invoke the alarm entry exactly as
 476:    `settings.json` spells it, through the same shell a hook runs in, and record what happens.
 477:    Then invoke it as `cmd /c "<path>"`. If the bare form fails and the `cmd /c` form works, the
 478:    hypothesis is confirmed and no further diagnosis is needed.
 479: 2. **If confirmed, fix `.claude/settings.json`**: both alarm entries invoke through `cmd /c`.
 480:    `.claude/settings.json` is in the declared territory. The measured 35.5 ms median already
 481:    reflects the `cmd /c` form, so the overhead number stands and step 6 needs no re-measurement â€”
 482:    confirm that rather than assume it.
 483: 3. **Then ONE probe re-run** to prove the corrected entries deliver. This is the run that closes
 484:    step 10 and moves step 9.
 485: 4. **If the bare form turns out to work standalone**, the hypothesis is wrong: STOP, do not fix
 486:    anything, report to me. I will rule again. Do not start characterizing the runtime on your own
 487:    initiative â€” that is a different investigation and it needs a ruling first.
 488: 5. If a genuine `PostToolUseFailure`-only gap survives a corrected success entry, D11's
 489:    pre-decided contingency finally applies as written: drop that ONE entry, keep the rest, record
